# Line endings.
#
# This repo is edited on Windows and RUN on Linux, and core.autocrlf=true has
# no opinion about which files must stay LF. A shell script is the case where
# that matters: bash on the VPS reads a CRLF line as ending in a literal \r,
# which surfaces as `$'\r': command not found` or `bad interpreter` — an error
# that points at the shell rather than at the line endings that caused it.
#
# Anything the server executes or reads as text is pinned to LF here so it
# cannot depend on which machine last touched it.
*.sh      text eol=lf
Caddyfile text eol=lf
*.service text eol=lf
*.sql     text eol=lf

# Windows-side tooling keeps CRLF.
*.ps1     text eol=crlf
*.cmd     text eol=crlf
*.bat     text eol=crlf
